<Project>

  <!--
    Central package management. Versions are declared once, here. Individual .csproj
    files reference packages WITHOUT a Version attribute.

    THREE RULES, all learned the hard way on CI:

    1. Every PackageReference needs a PackageVersion here. A missing one is NU1010 and
       fails restore for the entire solution, not just the offending project.

    2. Declare ONLY packages that a project actually references, plus deliberate security
       pins in the section at the bottom.

       Rule 2 is the non-obvious one. CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled (below) means
       every entry here is a hard pin, including on packages nothing referenced. An entry
       left over from a future phase then sits as a stale version floor, and the moment
       something in the dependency graph needs a higher version you get NU1109
       "Detected package downgrade" — a restore failure caused entirely by a package
       nobody uses.

       That is exactly what happened: Azure.Identity was pinned at 1.13.2 while
       Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 6.1.1 (via EF Core SqlServer and the SQL health check)
       requires >= 1.14.2. Twenty other speculative entries were removed at the same time
       to stop the same failure recurring one package at a time.

       Adding a package back is a one-line change at the point it is genuinely needed.

    3. Pin to the LATEST PATCH of the band, never to the band's .0 release, and move every
       package in a coordinated band together.

       Rule 3 is rule 2's twin, and it is what caused the third failed run. Everything in the
       Microsoft 10.0 band was pinned at 10.0.0 while the band had shipped through 10.0.11.
       A .0 pin is not "the safe conservative choice" — under transitive pinning it is the
       lowest possible floor, so it is the value most likely to sit underneath something
       else's requirement.

       The failure path was three hops and involved no package we chose directly:
         Azure.Identity 1.21.0
           -> Azure.Core 1.53.0
             -> Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions 10.0.3
               -> Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions >= 10.0.3   (pinned here at 10.0.0)
       and in parallel, Options 10.0.3 pulled Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection
       .Abstractions >= 10.0.3 against the same 10.0.0 pin.

       Pinning the whole band at the newest published patch closes this deterministically:
       nothing in a restored graph can require a version that does not exist yet.

       `tools/check-package-pins.py` checks this against nuget.org before you push. Dependabot
       (.github/dependabot.yml) keeps the band moving so a failed CI run is never how we find
       out that it drifted.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Entity Framework Core">
    <!--
      All four move together, always. EF Core SqlServer depends on EF Core Relational, which
      depends on EF Core — mixing patches inside this set is a self-inflicted NU1109.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="ASP.NET Core and identity">
    <!--
      3.8.2, not 3.7.1. GHSA-rpq8-q44m-2rpg (moderate, CVSS 4.7) affects
      Microsoft.Identity.Web >= 3.2.0 < 3.8.2 — client secrets and certificate details can
      reach application logs under some conditions.

      This bump also clears the companion advisory on Microsoft.Identity.Abstractions
      (affected >= 7.1.0 < 9.0.0) without a separate pin: Identity.Web 3.8.2 depends on
      Microsoft.Identity.Web.TokenAcquisition 3.8.2, which requires Abstractions >= 9.0.0.

      Deliberately the minimum patched 3.x rather than the current 4.14.2 — a patch-level
      move inside v3 is API-compatible with what is already written, and a major-version
      jump is not something to make blind while the build has never compiled. Moving to
      4.x is worth doing once CI is green and the change can actually be verified.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Identity.Web" Version="3.8.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Identity.Web.UI" Version="3.8.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.QuickGrid" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Azure">
    <!--
      1.21.0. Must be >= 1.14.2 to satisfy Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 6.1.1, which arrives
      via both Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer and AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer.
      The previous 1.13.2 pin was below that floor and produced NU1109 on every project
      that touched SQL.

      Taken to current stable rather than the bare minimum: being conservative about
      version floors is what caused the failure, and 1.21.0 carries no advisories.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Identity" Version="1.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Storage.Blobs" Version="12.23.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets" Version="4.7.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Cross-cutting">
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" Version="9.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.ApplicationInsights" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Enrichers.Environment" Version="3.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore" Version="2.22.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience" Version="9.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Extensions primitives">
    <!--
      Referenced explicitly by FcTelecom.Application, which is a plain class library and so
      does not get these from a shared framework the way the Web project does.

      These two are the most heavily depended-upon packages in the entire graph — almost
      everything Microsoft ships sits on top of them. They are therefore the two most likely
      to be pushed above whatever is pinned here, and they are exactly the two that failed on
      run three. They move with the rest of the 10.0 band, never independently.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Data formats">
    <PackageVersion Include="ClosedXML" Version="0.105.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="CsvHelper" Version="33.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Testing">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.12.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.0.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.2.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NetArchTest.Rules" Version="1.3.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Transitive security pins">
    <!--
      Pinned, NOT referenced — the one deliberate exception to rule 2 above.

      System.Security.Cryptography.Xml resolved to 9.0.0, which carries eight high-severity
      advisories (CVE-2026-26171, CVE-2026-50527 and others — uncontrolled resource
      consumption in EncryptedXml). NuGet Audit raises those as NU1903, and with
      TreatWarningsAsErrors on they fail restore. That is the audit working as intended.

      10.0.10 was the first patched version in the 10.0 band across all of them (9.0.18 is
      the 9.0-band equivalent if a downgrade is ever needed). Pinned at 10.0.11 to stay level
      with the rest of the band per rule 3 — a lone 10.0.10 here would be the next floor to
      sit underneath something.

      Revisit rather than leave forever: when whatever pulls this in ships a build that
      already depends on a patched version, delete the line.
      `dotnet nuget why <project> System.Security.Cryptography.Xml` names the source.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Removed because nothing references them (see rule 2). Re-add alongside the
    PackageReference that needs them:

      Phase 2 worker    Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker{,.Sdk,.ApplicationInsights},
                        Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.{Timer,Storage.Queues},
                        Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting, Azure.Storage.Queues
      Phase 2 alerting  Microsoft.Graph, Microsoft.Identity.Web.MicrosoftGraph
      Validation        FluentValidation, FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions
      Integration tests Testcontainers.MsSql, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing, NSubstitute
      E2E tests         Microsoft.Playwright
      Not yet needed    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer,
                        Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks,
                        Microsoft.Extensions.Options, Azure.Security.KeyVault.Keys,
                        Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.AzureAppConfiguration
  -->

</Project>
